---
/**
 * BothSides — homepage section 4, "Both sides of the table".
 *
 * DESIGN: B2 "Shared spine, both ends", Figma frame 78:1065 on page
 * `Section — Both sides` (78:3). Owner decision, 2026-08-02, recorded in
 * specs/HOMEPAGE-DECISION-TABLE.md. Six variants were rendered; this is the one
 * taken, and it is not the one I recommended. Build it, do not re-argue it.
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT THE GEOMETRY MAKES. One raised rail carries three station caps —
 * Requirement, Evaluation, After award. The supplier lane sits above it and
 * reads left to right; the authority lane sits below it and reads right to
 * left. Two readers, opposite directions, the same spine. That is the whole
 * claim: a commitment means the same thing from either chair. Any future edit
 * that flattens this into two side-by-side cards throws the argument away and
 * keeps the words.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY THE OLD WORDING IS NOT CARRIED OVER. The previous
 * build was two prose cards gated on OA-01, because the section once led with
 * "the only engine that sits on both sides" and closed with "almost nothing
 * else in this market does both" — an absolute uniqueness claim, stated twice,
 * with nothing behind it. B2 carries no competitor claim and no figures at all,
 * so there is nothing here to source and nothing to hedge. The National TOMs
 * form of words that the old cards needed is not needed here because this
 * section no longer describes the pricing mechanism; that hedge now lives in
 * full on /sources, under "National TOMs".
 *
 * NO ARROW GLYPHS IN MONO, and this is a real defect not a preference.
 * JetBrains Mono has no arrow coverage, so `→` falls back to a substitute glyph
 * and rendered as a stray `+` in the first build of this section. The lane
 * labels use `>>` and `<<`, which the face does have. Do not put an arrow
 * character inside anything using --font-mono anywhere on this site.
 *
 * THE ROWS ALIGN WITHOUT SUBGRID, deliberately. Each station is its own grid
 * with rows `1fr auto auto auto auto 1fr`. Stations are grid items in one row,
 * so they stretch to a common height; the four `auto` rows hold fixed-height
 * decoration, so the two `1fr` rows resolve to the same size in every station.
 * The rail therefore lands on the same y in all three columns by construction,
 * whatever the copy does. Subgrid would also work and is ~93% supported; this
 * needs no fallback path at all.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT, AND NOTHING WAITING ON AN OBSERVER. Every word is in the HTML
 * and painted at first render. This section is one of the four that once
 * shipped permanently invisible on this site because their content was gated on
 * an IntersectionObserver that never fired.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Station {
  /** The station cap: one moment in the same contract. */
  name: string;
  /** Read from above, left to right. */
  supplier: string;
  /** Read from below, right to left. */
  authority: string;
  /**
   * A trailing clause marked as refused rather than verified. Held as its own
   * field rather than matched out of `authority` at render time: a string
   * replace would fail silently the first time somebody reworded one of the two
   * and not the other, and it would fail by dropping the highlight, which is
   * the one thing on this rail carrying meaning. Orchid is the refusal colour
   * sitewide and is never used decoratively.
   */
  refused?: string;
}

/**
 * THREE MOMENTS, NOT THREE STEPS, so they are not numbered. This is the same
 * contract seen at three points on one timeline; a 01/02/03 rail would assert a
 * sequence the reader has to follow. ReasoningTrace numbers its five steps
 * because those genuinely are ordered — you cannot check a figure you have not
 * computed.
 */
const STATIONS: Station[] = [
  {
    name: 'Requirement',
    supplier: 'Which measures apply',
    authority: 'Publish the rubric',
  },
  {
    name: 'Evaluation',
    supplier: 'Answer from your library',
    authority: 'Located word for word, ',
    refused: 'or dropped',
  },
  {
    name: 'After award',
    supplier: 'Deliver what was priced',
    authority: 'Audit the commitment',
  },
];

/*
 * THE PROOF ROW IS GONE, AND SO IS THE CLOSING LINE (owner review, 2026-08-02,
 * second pass: "Same issues with 'Both sides of the table' ... we are giving so
 * much textual information", and the verdict that the page reads as research
 * rather than product).
 *
 * A mono row reading "PROOF POINT · UK PUBLIC · PROCUREMENT ACT 2023 s.52 · PPN
 * 002 · NATIONAL TOMS CONVENTIONS" is a citation strip, and the standing rule is
 * now that the homepage carries none. Every one of those four instruments is on
 * /sources with its dates, its threshold and a primary-source link, which is
 * more than this row ever gave: it named them and stopped.
 *
 * The closing line, "The same measures, whichever chair you read them from",
 * restated the heading and the standfirst in a third form. Cover it with your
 * hand and the rail read from both ends still makes the point, so it is
 * commentary and it went.
 *
 * s.52 and s.71 remain separate duties everywhere they appear (OA-26). Nothing
 * about that changed here, because nothing here names either of them any more.
 */
---

{/*
  NO STANDFIRST. It said in three clauses what the two lane labels, the arrow
  directions and the rail say by construction. The heading names the idea and the
  drawing performs it, which is the test this pass applied to every section.
*/}
<Section
  eyebrow="Both sides of the table"
  title="Read from either end."
  id="both-sides"
>
  {/*
    `data-lit` at threshold 0.35, per `146:3`. Rail drawn, three caps lit, both
    lanes readable before this attribute means anything. If motion.ts never
    runs it stays unset and the section is the finished diagram.
  */}
  <div class="bs" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.35">
    {/*
      THE SECTION'S ARGUMENT, LIT RATHER THAN CAPTIONED (`146:174`). The
      supplier lane is keyed teal from ABOVE and the authority lane violet from
      BELOW: two sources on opposite sides of one spine, which is the same claim
      the heading makes. Neither hue marks anything — swap them and the section
      still says two readers, opposite directions, same rail — so both are light
      and the palette rule is untouched.
    */}
    <div class="bs__amb" aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="bs__amb-sup"></b>
      <b class="bs__amb-auth"></b>
    </div>

    {/*
      The lane label is the diagram's only orientation cue, so the direction is
      written as words for a screen reader and drawn as chevrons for a sighted
      reader. The chevrons are aria-hidden: `>>` announced literally is noise.
    */}
    <p class="bs__lane bs__lane--sup">
      Supplier reads<span class="bs__dir" aria-hidden="true">&gt;&gt;</span>
      <span class="visually-hidden">left to right, from above the spine</span>
    </p>

    <ol class="bs__spine">
      {
        STATIONS.map((s, i) => (
          <li class="bs__station" data-mid={i === 1 ? '' : undefined}>
            {/*
              A PARAGRAPH, NOT AN <h3>, AND THE TAG NOW MATCHES WHAT IT IS.
              Sitewide type audit, 2026-08-02: this was the last off-scale
              heading recipe on the site, an h3 rendered at --t-micro, weight
              500, mono, uppercase, inside a pill. Two fixes were available:
              size it as a heading, or stop calling it one.

              It is not a heading. A heading introduces the content beneath it,
              and this chip does not: it is the station's NAME on a rail, and
              the two readings either side of it belong to the station rather
              than being introduced by its label. Each reading already carries
              its own visually-hidden "Supplier:" or "Authority:" prefix, and
              the <ol>/<li> structure carries the grouping, so nothing is lost
              from the accessibility tree by dropping the heading level. What is
              gained is that the site now has four heading sizes and no fifth
              recipe hiding behind a pill.
            */}
            <p class="bs__chip">{s.name}</p>

            <p class="bs__read bs__read--sup">
              <span class="visually-hidden">Supplier: </span>
              {s.supplier}
            </p>

            {/* Decoration. The rail and its cap carry no information that the
                text above and below does not already carry in full. */}
            <span class="bs__tick bs__tick--up" aria-hidden="true" />
            <span class="bs__railcell" aria-hidden="true">
              {/*
                TWO LIGHTS, ONE PER READER, AND THE RAIL IS ALREADY DRAWN UNDER
                THEM. Each station owns its own third of both travels: A runs
                left to right and takes its third at 0, 800 and 1,600ms; B runs
                right to left and takes the same thirds in the opposite order.
                Three equal cells, one clock, so the two cross dead centre at
                1,200ms without either of them knowing the rail's total width.
                That is why this is per-cell rather than one overlay: the rail's
                y position falls out of a grid nothing can measure in CSS.
              */}
              <span class="bs__track">
                <span class="bs__beam bs__beam--a" />
                <span class="bs__beam bs__beam--b" />
              </span>
              <span class="bs__cap" />
            </span>
            <span class="bs__tick bs__tick--down" aria-hidden="true" />

            <p class="bs__read bs__read--auth">
              <span class="visually-hidden">Authority: </span>
              {s.authority}
              {s.refused && <span class="bs__refused">{s.refused}</span>}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <p class="bs__lane bs__lane--auth">
      <span class="bs__dir" aria-hidden="true">&lt;&lt;</span>Authority reads
      <span class="visually-hidden">right to left, from below the spine</span>
    </p>

    {/*
      DEPTH BEHIND THE DIAGRAM. The Figma shows a single "Learn more", and the
      standing target for this pass is one link per section. This is the one
      section with a DOCUMENTED reason to carry two: it has two audiences, the
      whole argument is that they are different chairs, and a single generic
      link cannot serve both. Dropping the buyer link would also strand
      /crowmark-buyers, which the content-parity allow-list names as the page
      still carrying the buyer proposition in full. Two links, one per lane, and
      each names its own destination rather than saying "Learn more".
    */}
    <p class="bs__more">
      <a href="/crowmark">For suppliers</a>
      <a href="/crowmark-buyers">For buyers</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .bs {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* ── Ambient: two keys, opposite sides ───────────────────────────────────*/
  /* Bleed exactly one gutter, never a percentage. See MarketShape.astro for
     the arithmetic: a percentage bleed overflowed the page at 390 and 768. */
  .bs__amb {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: -18%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* All four edges are masked. `overflow: hidden` stops the bleed widening
       the page but cuts a 150px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in
       a straight line draws a visible lighter rectangle. One radial mask fades
       every edge at once, so there is no axis for it to miss. See the full note
       in MarketShape.astro, including the mistake of masking only one axis.  */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      115% 105% at 50% 50%,
      rgb(0 0 0) 48%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(
      115% 105% at 50% 50%,
      rgb(0 0 0) 48%,
      transparent 100%
    );
  }

  .bs__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* Teal from above, with the supplier. */
  .bs__amb-sup {
    width: 620px;
    height: 380px;
    left: 2%;
    top: -22%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  /* Violet from below, with the authority. */
  .bs__amb-auth {
    width: 600px;
    height: 380px;
    right: 2%;
    bottom: -24%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .bs__amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 260px; }
  }

  /* ── Lane labels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .bs__lane {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .bs__lane--sup { justify-content: flex-start; }

  /*
   * The bottom lane label needs its own clearance and the top one does not.
   * The spine's first row is `1fr` with the supplier text pushed to its bottom
   * edge, so there is always slack above it; the last row is `1fr` with the
   * authority text pushed to its TOP edge, so the label lands directly against
   * the final line. Measured at 1440: 9px between "the commitment" and the
   * label, which reads as a collision rather than a lane marker.
   */
  .bs__lane--auth {
    margin-top: var(--stack-2);
    justify-content: flex-end;
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  /* `>>` and `<<`, never `→`. JetBrains Mono carries no arrow glyphs and the
     fallback renders as a stray `+`. */
  .bs__dir { letter-spacing: 0.06em; }

  /* ── The spine ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .bs__spine {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  /*
   * ROW ALIGNMENT WITHOUT SUBGRID. The four `auto` rows are fixed-height
   * decoration, identical in every station, and the stations stretch to a
   * common height as grid items. The two `1fr` rows therefore resolve to the
   * same height in all three columns, which puts the rail on one line however
   * the copy wraps.
   */
  .bs__station {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: 1fr auto auto auto auto 1fr;
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    /* Two stations' text must not touch at the column boundary. */
    padding-inline: var(--stack-1);
  }

  .bs__read {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 22ch;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.45;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .bs__read--sup {
    grid-row: 1;
    align-self: end;
    padding-bottom: var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* Teal is the verification colour and the authority is the party verifying,
     so the lane colour is doing semantic work rather than distinguishing two
     things by hue alone. The visually-hidden "Authority:" prefix carries the
     same distinction with all colour removed — WCAG 1.4.1. */
  .bs__read--auth {
    grid-row: 6;
    align-self: start;
    padding-top: var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  /* Orchid = refused, sitewide, and it never appears decoratively. */
  .bs__refused { color: var(--c-orchid); }

  .bs__tick {
    width: 1px;
    height: 26px;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 45%, transparent)
    );
  }

  .bs__tick--up { grid-row: 2; }

  .bs__tick--down {
    grid-row: 5;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 45%, transparent),
      transparent
    );
  }

  /*
   * THE RAIL IS DRAWN PER STATION AND MEETS ITSELF. Each cell's line is 100% of
   * its column, so three cells butt together into one continuous rail with no
   * absolute positioning and nothing to re-measure. The outer two fade at the
   * outer edge, which is what gives the rail its raised, lit look rather than
   * ending in two hard stops.
   */
  .bs__railcell {
    grid-row: 3;
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    /* Full column width, escaping the station's own inline padding. */
    width: calc(100% + 2 * var(--stack-1));
    height: 14px;
  }

  .bs__railcell::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    top: 50%;
    height: 1px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 55%, transparent);
  }

  .bs__station:first-child .bs__railcell::before {
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      transparent,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 55%, transparent)
    );
  }

  .bs__station:last-child .bs__railcell::before {
    background: linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 55%, transparent),
      transparent
    );
  }

  /*
   * THE ISOMETRIC CAP. A flattened ellipse lit from above: a light-to-dark wash
   * rather than a flat fill, which is the single strongest tell separating a
   * drawn object from a PowerPoint shape. The teal halo puts it on the rail
   * rather than in front of it.
   */
  .bs__cap {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    width: 22px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 62%, transparent),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 14%, transparent)
    );
    box-shadow:
      0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 10%, transparent),
      0 0 14px var(--c-teal-glow);
  }

  /* ══ M4 · TWO LIGHTS, ONE SPINE ═══════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `146:2` — "Two readers, opposite directions, meeting on the same
   * rail". TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.35. DURATION 2,400ms.
   * PLAYS ONCE per page load: prose sits above and below this diagram, so it
   * plays and holds rather than looping past a reader mid-sentence.
   * REDUCED MOTION rail and caps at rest brightness, no travel.
   *
   * BOTH LIGHTS ARE WHITE-CORED, so neither can be read as a marker even though
   * one is teal and the other violet. `141:2` L04. The rail underneath them is
   * drawn at full strength from first paint and is never touched.
   *
   * ONE DEVIATION FROM THE BOARD, AND IT IS THE HONEST ONE. `146:90` puts the
   * crossing at 1,100ms. Three equal cells at 800ms each cross at exactly
   * 1,200ms, and the board's own beats note says the cap bloom "fires on the
   * crossing, not on a timer". So the crossing wins and the bloom is at 1,200ms.
   */
  /*
   * THE TRACK EXISTS TO CLIP. Each beam is a full-width element translating
   * from -50% to +50%, so it is half a column outside its cell at each end of
   * the travel. Unclipped, that grows the page's scrollWidth for as long as the
   * light is moving — a horizontal-overflow failure that only exists mid-
   * animation and is invisible to any check that measures a settled page.
   *
   * The track is NOT the railcell itself. The railcell also holds .bs__cap,
   * whose 14px teal halo is drawn with a box-shadow, and clipping the railcell
   * would cut that halo off square.
   */
  .bs__track {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    /* Centred on the rail, with headroom either side for the beams' own glow.
       A 1px track would clip a 10px box-shadow to nothing. */
    top: 50%;
    height: 18px;
    margin-top: -9px;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .bs__beam {
    /* No animation: no pixels. The rest state of both lights is nothing. */
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .bs__beam {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      inset-inline: 0;
      top: 9px;
      height: 1px;
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* The band is drawn in the middle of a full-width element and the element
       translates, so the travel is one compositor transform and the light
       scales with the column rather than being a fixed px width that reads
       wide on a phone. */
    .bs__beam--a {
      background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent 34%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 70%, transparent) 44%,
        var(--c-text) 50%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 70%, transparent) 56%,
        transparent 66%
      );
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-teal-glow);
    }

    .bs__beam--b {
      background: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent 34%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 70%, transparent) 44%,
        var(--c-text) 50%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 70%, transparent) 56%,
        transparent 66%
      );
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-violet) 45%, transparent);
    }

    [data-lit='on'] .bs__beam--a {
      animation: bs-beam-a 800ms linear forwards;
    }

    [data-lit='on'] .bs__beam--b {
      animation: bs-beam-b 800ms linear forwards;
    }

    /* A takes the stations left to right; B takes them right to left. One
       clock, mirrored, so the two are always the same distance from centre. */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(2) .bs__beam--a { animation-delay: 800ms; }
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(3) .bs__beam--a { animation-delay: 1600ms; }
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(2) .bs__beam--b { animation-delay: 800ms; }
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station:nth-child(1) .bs__beam--b { animation-delay: 1600ms; }

    /* Evaluation only, on the crossing. The beat the standfirst used to have to
       describe in words: the same measures, whichever chair you read them from. */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__station[data-mid] .bs__cap {
      animation: bs-cap 300ms var(--ease) 1200ms 1;
    }

    /*
     * THE REFUSAL, AND IT STAYS A MARKER. "or dropped" takes a 400ms orchid
     * glow as the two lights cross, which is the only orchid in the frame. It
     * is a flat orchid word with a glow BEHIND it and never a white-cored
     * light: that is the whole distinction the page teaches, and this section
     * is where it is spent.
     *
     * THE TEXT'S OWN OPACITY AND COLOUR ARE UNTOUCHED. The board specifies
     * 0.5 -> 0.95 -> 0.6, which would leave the word dimmer at the end than the
     * rest state it started from and breaks binding rule 02 on its own board.
     * A text-shadow rises from nothing and returns to nothing instead, so the
     * word measures identically against the background at every frame.
     */
    [data-lit='on'] .bs__refused {
      animation: bs-refused 400ms var(--ease) 1700ms 1;
    }
  }

  @keyframes bs-beam-a {
    from { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
    12%  { opacity: 1; }
    88%  { opacity: 1; }
    to   { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  @keyframes bs-beam-b {
    from { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
    12%  { opacity: 1; }
    88%  { opacity: 1; }
    to   { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* Scale about the cap's own centre. It is already translate(-50%,-50%) for
     positioning, so the scale has to be composed with that translate or the
     cap jumps a half-width sideways the moment the animation starts. */
  @keyframes bs-cap {
    0%   { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
    50%  {
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.08);
      box-shadow:
        0 0 0 1px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 10%, transparent),
        0 0 22px var(--c-teal-glow);
    }
    100% { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
  }

  @keyframes bs-refused {
    0%   { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-orchid) 0%, transparent); }
    45%  { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-orchid) 62%, transparent); }
    100% { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-orchid) 0%, transparent); }
  }

  .bs__chip {
    grid-row: 4;
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    background: var(--c-card);
  }

  /* ── Close ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The closing line and the mono proof row are gone, and their rules with
     them: see the note above STATIONS for why each went and where its content
     now lives. The links close the section directly, so they take the section
     gap the foot used to. */
  .bs__more {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0 var(--stack-2);
  }

  .bs__more a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-teal);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .bs__more a:hover { color: var(--c-teal-dark); }

  .bs__more a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal);
    outline-offset: 3px;
